To use a cache inside of a pipeline in Text::Format::Simple, you can create a closure that wraps the function you want to call and cache the result. Here's an example:
```
use Text::Format::Simple;

my $cache = {};
{
    my $wrapper = undef;
    $wrapper = sub {
        my ($input) = @_;
        return $cache{$input};
    };

    $cache = {}; # reset cache every time $wrapper is called

    $wrapper("foo") = "bar";

    foreach my $line (@pipeline) {
        my $output = &<wrapper>($line);
        format_line($output);
    }
}
```
In this example, `$wrapper` is a closure that takes one argument and checks if the result is already cached in `$cache`. If not, it returns whatever value it would have otherwise returned. The `$cache` is reset every time `$wrapper` is called by wrapping it in a block that initializes it.

In this example, we are iterating over `@pipeline` and formatting each line with Text::Format::Simple. We use the `$wrapper` closure to cache the result of each line, so it doesn't have to format it multiple times if needed.

Note that you may need to pass a copy of `$cache` to each iteration of the pipeline to ensure that it's not modified outside of the closure. You can do this by using the % keyword to modify it as a hash reference:
```
$cache = borrow(\\@cache); # borrow reference to avoid passing
```
